import sys
from awsglue.context import GlueContext
from pyspark.context import SparkContext

# Initialize Glue context
sc = SparkContext()
glueContext = GlueContext(sc)
spark = glueContext.spark_session

# S3 path to your Parquet file
s3_path = "s3://your-bucket-name/path/to/file.parquet"

# Read the Parquet file
df = spark.read.parquet(s3_path)

# Show the schema
df.printSchema()

# Optional: show first few rows
df.show(5)
# Convert schema to JSON
schema_json = df.schema.json()
schema_dict = json.loads(schema_json)

# Pretty-print JSON (optional)
pretty_schema = json.dumps(schema_dict, indent=4)
print(pretty_schema)

# Save schema JSON to S3
s3_output_path = "s3://your-bucket-name/path/to/schema.json"
spark.sparkContext.parallelize([pretty_schema]).saveAsTextFile(s3_output_path)





Top-Level Fields

dataset_name

Name of the target dataset (curated version).

Here: "financial_transactions_curated"

description

Human-readable description of the dataset and its purpose.

Useful for documentation.

fields

An array of field-level mappings between source and target.

Each item represents one column/field and includes metadata, validation rules, and mapping info.

created_date

Date when this metadata mapping was created or updated.

Useful for versioning and tracking changes.

Field-Level Metadata

Each field object has these keys:

field_name

Name of the field in the target dataset.

Example: "transaction_id"

source_field

Name of the field in the source/raw dataset.

If the field is newly added, it’s "-"

target_field

Name of the field in the target dataset. Usually same as field_name.

source_type

Data type of the field in the source dataset.

Example: "decimal(10,2)"

target_type

Data type of the field in the target dataset.

Can differ from source if type conversion happens.

nullable

Indicates whether this field can have NULL values in the target dataset.

Example: false means mandatory.

primary_key

Whether the field is part of the primary key in the target dataset.

Example: "transaction_id" is the primary key.

allowed_values

Optional enumeration of valid values.

Example: "transaction_type" can only be "debit" or "credit".

constraints

Any numeric or range constraints.

Example: "amount" has min 0.01 and max 1000000.00.

change_type

Indicates how the field changed from source to target. Options include:

"renamed" → name changed

"type_modified" → data type changed

"added" → new field in target dataset

Example Breakdown: transaction_type Field
{
  "field_name": "transaction_type",
  "source_field": "-",
  "target_field": "transaction_type",
  "source_type": "-",
  "target_type": "string",
  "nullable": false,
  "primary_key": false,
  "allowed_values": ["debit", "credit"],
  "constraints": null,
  "change_type": "added"
}


Explanation:

This field does not exist in source (source_field: "-")

Added in the target dataset (change_type: "added")

It’s mandatory (nullable: false)

Only allows "debit" or "credit" as valid values (allowed_values)

Features of this Glue Script

Dynamic field mapping from a JSON mapping file:

Handles renames, type conversions, and new fields.

Validation rules:

Null checks, numeric constraints, allowed values for categorical fields.

Partitioned write for optimized storage and query performance.

Fully reusable:

Update the JSON file, and the Glue job adapts automatically to schema changes.
